# Deploy a Microservice on Amazon EKS — Step by Step

A complete, beginner-friendly walkthrough. We will:

1. Install the tools we need
2. Create an EKS (Kubernetes) cluster on AWS named **`demo-test`**
3. Build a tiny microservice and push it to AWS
4. Deploy it to the cluster
5. Open it to the internet and test it
6. Scale it and clean everything up

Each step explains *what* you are doing and *why*, so you can follow along even if Kubernetes is new to you.

> **Quick mental model:**
> - **EKS** = AWS runs the Kubernetes "brain" (control plane) for you.
> - **Nodes** = EC2 servers where your app actually runs.
> - **Pod** = one running copy of your app.
> - **Deployment** = tells Kubernetes "keep N copies of my app alive."
> - **Service** = a stable address so traffic can reach your pods.

---

## Step 0 — What you need before starting

- An **AWS account** with permission to create EKS, EC2, IAM, VPC, ECR, and CloudFormation resources.
- A computer with a terminal (Linux, macOS, or WSL on Windows).
- About **30–40 minutes** (most of it is just waiting for AWS to build things).

You also need **four command-line tools**. We install them in Step 1.

| Tool | What it does |
|------|--------------|
| `aws` | Talks to your AWS account |
| `eksctl` | Creates the EKS cluster with one command |
| `kubectl` | Sends commands to your Kubernetes cluster |
| `docker` | Builds your app into a container image |

---

## Step 1 — Install the tools

Run the block that matches your operating system.

### macOS (using Homebrew)

```bash
brew install awscli eksctl kubernetes-cli
# Docker Desktop: download from https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop
```

### Linux (Ubuntu / Debian)

```bash
# AWS CLI
curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o awscliv2.zip
unzip awscliv2.zip && sudo ./aws/install

# eksctl
curl --silent --location "https://github.com/eksctl-io/eksctl/releases/latest/download/eksctl_$(uname -s)_amd64.tar.gz" | tar xz -C /tmp
sudo mv /tmp/eksctl /usr/local/bin

# kubectl
curl -LO "https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl"
sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 kubectl /usr/local/bin/kubectl

# docker
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y docker.io

# Allow running docker WITHOUT sudo (required — otherwise all docker commands fail)
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
newgrp docker          # applies the group change in the current terminal
```

### Check everything is installed

```bash
aws --version
eksctl version
kubectl version --client
docker --version
```

If each command prints a version number, you are good.

