## Step 4 — Build a tiny microservice

We'll make a minimal web service that returns a message. You don't need to know Node.js — just create these files.

Make a folder and go into it:

```bash
mkdir my-microservice && cd my-microservice
```

### File 1: `app.js` (the actual service)

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({
    message: 'Hello from my microservice on EKS!',
    hostname: require('os').hostname()  // shows which pod answered
  }));
});

server.listen(3000, () => console.log('Service running on port 3000'));
```

### File 2: `Dockerfile` (recipe to package the app)

A **Dockerfile** is a set of instructions that turns your code into a portable "container image" — a self-contained box with your app and everything it needs to run.

```dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine
WORKDIR /app
COPY app.js .
EXPOSE 3000
CMD ["node", "app.js"]
```

**Line by line:** start from a small Node.js base image → set a working folder → copy our code in → note that the app uses port 3000 → run the app.

