## Step 5 — Push the image to AWS (ECR)

The cluster can't see images on your laptop. We store the image in **ECR (Elastic Container Registry)** — AWS's private image storage — so the cluster can pull it.

### 5a. Create a repository

```bash
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name my-microservice --region ap-south-1
```

### 5b. Set a variable for your registry address

This grabs your account ID automatically so you don't have to type it.

```bash
ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)
REGION=ap-south-1
REGISTRY=$ACCOUNT_ID.dkr.ecr.$REGION.amazonaws.com
echo $REGISTRY
```

### 5c. Log Docker in to ECR

```bash
aws ecr get-login-password --region $REGION | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin $REGISTRY
```

### 5d. Build, tag, and push the image

```bash
# Build the image from your Dockerfile
docker build -t my-microservice .

# Tag it with the full ECR address
docker tag my-microservice:latest $REGISTRY/my-microservice:latest

# Push it to AWS
docker push $REGISTRY/my-microservice:latest
```

> 💡 If you are on a Mac with an M-series chip (ARM), build for the cluster's architecture with:
> `docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t my-microservice .`

---

## Step 6 — Write the Kubernetes instructions

Kubernetes is told what to do using **YAML files**. We need two:

- a **Deployment** (run my app, keep 2 copies alive)
- a **Service** (give it a public web address)

### File: `deployment.yaml`

First, create the file with a placeholder image name:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: my-microservice
spec:
  replicas: 2                       # run 2 copies (pods)
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-microservice
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-microservice
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: my-microservice
        image: REGISTRY_PLACEHOLDER/my-microservice:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
        resources:                  # polite limits so it doesn't hog the node
          requests:
            cpu: "100m"
            memory: "128Mi"
          limits:
            cpu: "250m"
            memory: "256Mi"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: my-microservice
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer              # asks AWS for a public load balancer
  selector:
    app: my-microservice          # send traffic to pods with this label
  ports:
  - port: 80                      # the public port
    targetPort: 3000              # the app's port inside the pod
```

Then run this command to automatically insert your real registry address into the file:

```bash
# Safety check — make sure $REGISTRY is not empty before running sed
# If this prints nothing, go back and re-run Step 5b first
echo "Registry: $REGISTRY"

# Replace REGISTRY_PLACEHOLDER with your actual ECR registry URL
# Note: sed -i.bak works on BOTH Linux and macOS (plain sed -i fails on macOS)
sed -i.bak "s|REGISTRY_PLACEHOLDER|$REGISTRY|g" deployment.yaml

# Confirm it looks right — you should see your account ID in the image line
grep "image:" deployment.yaml
```

The output should look like:
`image: 123456789012.dkr.ecr.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/my-microservice:latest`

If the line shows `image: /my-microservice:latest` (nothing before the `/`), your `$REGISTRY` was empty — re-run the variable assignments in Step 5b, then repeat the `sed` command.

> 💡 Shell variables like `$REGISTRY` disappear when you close the terminal. If you opened a new terminal since Step 5b, re-run those four lines before running `sed`.

**The key idea:** the Service finds pods using the `app: my-microservice` label, and `type: LoadBalancer` makes AWS automatically create a real load balancer with a public URL.

---

## Step 7 — Deploy it

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

Watch the pods start up (press `Ctrl+C` to stop watching once they're `Running`):

```bash
kubectl get pods -w
```

You should see 2 pods reach status `Running`.

---

## Step 8 — Get your public URL and test it

Creating the load balancer takes 2–3 minutes. Check for the address:

```bash
kubectl get service my-microservice
```

Look under the `EXTERNAL-IP` column for a long AWS hostname like
`a1b2c3...ap-south-1.elb.amazonaws.com`. Once it appears (not `<pending>`), test it:

```bash
curl http://<EXTERNAL-IP>
```

You should get back:

```json
{"message":"Hello from my microservice on EKS!","hostname":"my-microservice-xxxx"}
```

🎉 **Your microservice is live on the internet, running on EKS.** Run `curl` a few times — the `hostname` will change between your two pods, proving the load balancer is sharing traffic.

