## Step 9 — Useful day-to-day commands

```bash
# See everything you created
kubectl get all

# Read the logs from your app
kubectl logs -l app=my-microservice

# Scale up to 5 copies (handles more traffic)
kubectl scale deployment my-microservice --replicas=5

# Ship a new version after rebuilding + pushing the image
kubectl rollout restart deployment my-microservice

# Describe a pod if something looks wrong
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
```

---

## Step 10 — Clean up (IMPORTANT — avoid surprise bills)

An EKS cluster costs money every hour it exists, even when idle. When you're done:

```bash
# 1. Delete the app first (this also removes the AWS load balancer)
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml

# 2. Delete the whole cluster
eksctl delete cluster --name demo-test --region ap-south-1

# 3. (Optional) Delete the image repository
aws ecr delete-repository --repository-name my-microservice --region ap-south-1 --force
```

> ⚠️ **Always delete the Service/LoadBalancer before deleting the cluster.** If you skip step 1, the load balancer can be left behind and keep charging you.

